    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "**Edit:** Apparently the end-inclusive nature of `Range:has` [is not intentional behavior](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/41225#issuecomment-5289654338). Original issue description below.\n\n### Problem\n\nWhen checking to see if the cursor is inside of a `vim.Range`, the inclusive nature of `vim.Range:has()` will incorrectly include positions where the cursor is directly after the bounds of the range, since the end column will be the same as the cursor's column:\n\n```lua\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange:has(vim.pos.cursor()) -- Logic error: Will include positions where `vim.pos.cursor().col == range.end_col`!\n```\n\nThe best way I've come up with is this, which is very verbose:\n\n```lua\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange = vim.range(range.buf, range.start_row, range.start_col, range.end_row, range.end_col - 1)\nrange:has(vim.pos.cursor()) \n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nProvide some method to deal with this in code. Potential solutions:\n\n```lua\n--- Modifiable ranges\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange.end_col = range.end_col - 1\nrange:has(vim.pos.cursor())\n\n-- Method to create a new range from offsets\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange:shift(0, 0, 0, -1):has(vim.pos.cursor())\n\n-- Add option to Range:has()\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange:has(vim.pos.cursor(), { end_exclusive = true ))\n```\n\n @ofseed @TheLeoP",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "The last time that I considered distinguishing between inclusive and exclusive `vim.Pos`, the code ended up being a giant mess and my personal conclusion was that it made no sense to have that kind of logic inside of `vim.Pos` and have it only inside of `vim.Range` (where start is always inclusive and end is always exclusive). So, I've ended up creating `vim.Range` objects that represent empty ranges when I want to compare a `vim.Pos` to the end of a `vim.Range`.\n\n> -- Method to create a new range from offsets\n\nI don't like this solution for this use case, but I can see it working for things like \"I want to get a range/pos just before/after another range/pos\".\n\n> -- Add option to Range:has()\n\nThis one seems like the cleaner option to me, but it would be an option only for the `vim.Pos` overload. So, we could use a name like `end_exclusive_pos` and throw an error if it's used with a `vim.Range`",
